# REVISION-ANDROID-RELEASE-READINESS-PHASE4A-PILOT-AUTHORITY-1 — durable rules

Gate: `php artisan android:release-readiness --strict`.
Config: `config/android_release.php`.
Scanner: `app/Support/Android/AndroidReleaseGovernanceScanner.php`.
Extends rule `141-android-production-release-governance.mdc` §17. Nothing in
rule 141 or 142 is withdrawn.

## 1. A root-only green is not green

The gate must reach `GO` when run by the **canonical production runtime
identity** (`daengtisiams`), not only by `root`. A control that passes only for
the privileged account trains operators to run audits as root, which is the
opposite of what INFRA-SEC-RUNTIME-1 bought.

Root must keep working too. It simply stops being the workaround that makes
readiness reportable.

## 2. The root-owned deployed tree stays root-owned

INFRA-SEC-RUNTIME-1 deliberately pairs a root-owned checkout with an
unprivileged application runtime. That is the boundary functioning. Git's
foreign-ownership refusal is a **consequence of the boundary, not a defect in
it**, and the repository is not to be chowned to make an audit green.

## 3. Answer the refusal narrowly, never disable it

The scanner passes an **invocation-scoped** `-c safe.directory=<the repository
it was constructed to audit>` and nothing else. All of the following are
forbidden:

- `safe.directory=*`, in any scope.
- A persistent `--global` / `--system` entry written by a script, a deploy step
  or a runbook instruction.
- Any change that makes running the audit as root the supported path.
- Disabling git's ownership check by any other means.

Verified behaviour, on production git 2.43.0 and development git 2.53.0:
the command scope is protected configuration, so `-c safe.directory=<path>` is
honoured; the same option naming a **different** path is still refused; and a
`safe.directory` entry for a parent directory does **not** trust a nested
repository. The trust is genuinely one repository.

## 4. The path is an argument, never a shell string

Git is invoked with an argument array, so `-c`, `safe.directory=<path>` and the
subcommand are separate argv entries and no path is ever parsed by a shell. The
path comes from the container binding (`base_path()`); it is not, and must not
become, request input.

## 5. Every other git failure stays closed

If the index cannot be read, `no_committed_key_material` is **FAIL**. Absence of
evidence is never evidence of absence, and a scanner that reports "clean"
because it could not look is worse than no scanner. This applies to every
failure mode except the one narrow ownership case above, which is answered
rather than tolerated.

## 6. Say which failure happened

Checks carry a `reason`, because an unreadable index and a real finding are the
same status and completely different incidents:

- `index_access_error` — git could not read the index. No conclusion about
  contents was reached.
- `forbidden_key_material_found` — the index was read and a forbidden artifact
  is tracked.
- `not_tracked` — the index was read and the file genuinely is not in it.
- `clean` / `tracked` — the index was read and the expected state holds.

`gradle_wrapper_executable` must never say a file "is not tracked by git" when
git refused to open the repository. That message cost a full diagnosis once.

## 7. Phase 4A pilot authority — recorded, not exercised

`enforcement.owner_signoff` records the owner's decision:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| `phase_4a_pilot_authorized` | `true` |
| `authorized_scope` | `pilot_branch_or_device` — **never** `global_doctor_enforcement` |
| `pilot_doctor` | `drg Karmila` |
| `pilot_branch` | `Cabang Sunu` |
| `rollback_owner` | `Custodian 1 / Raushan Fikri Ridha / IT` |
| `pilot_activated` | `false` |

Human authority is recorded **as written**, not as production row ids. Resolving
those names to real records belongs to the phase that actually runs the pilot;
an id copied between environments is how the wrong Doctor gets enrolled.

## 8. Authorization is not activation

Recording a sign-off moves nothing. After this revision, and until a later phase
explicitly enables the pilot:

- `enforcement.active` = `false`
- `enforcement.doctor_browser_login_denied` = `false`
- `enforcement.current_stage` = `off`
- `doctor.trusted_device_enforcement` defaults to `false`
- no `DoctorDeviceAuthorization` row exists because of this sign-off
- **no** Doctor, pilot or otherwise, is enforced

The rollback owner may disable a pilot and return the ladder to `off` at any
time. That is the whole point of naming one before starting.

## 9. Signing is untouched by this authority

Phase 4A authorization says nothing about keys. `production_certificate_sha256`
stays `null`, no production signing key is provisioned, custody remains
**PARTIAL** (Custodian 1 established; Custodian 2, offsite backup, Custodian 3
and sealed-cold backup deferred by explicit owner decision), and
`android:verify-release` still fails closed with no authority to verify against.

The gate reporting `GO` means **repository release governance** is in order. It
has never meant a signed production APK exists, and both
`PRODUCTION_SIGNING_KEY_PROVISIONED=false` and
`ANDROID_REAL_DEVICE_VALIDATION=false` are printed on every run precisely so
that inference cannot be made.

## 10. History stays historical

Phase 4 and the first Phase 4A attempt are recorded **BLOCKED / NO-GO** and are
not rewritten as successes. This revision is a prerequisite cleanup and a
governance record, not a replacement attempt, and it creates neither
`feature-doctor-trusted-android-device-lock-1-phase-4a-app-only-real-device-pilot-go`
nor `feature-doctor-trusted-android-device-lock-1-go`.
